mod('water','Water & hydrogen bonds',
 'Water is a small molecule that behaves like a much bigger one. Almost every property life relies on comes from one weak attraction between water molecules.',
 ['A water molecule is <b>polar</b>: oxygen pulls electrons harder than hydrogen does, so oxygen is slightly negative and the hydrogens slightly positive. The positive hydrogen of one molecule is attracted to the negative oxygen of another. This weak attraction is a <b>hydrogen bond</b>. One bond is weak and breaks easily, but liquid water has enormous numbers of them forming and breaking constantly.',
  'That network explains the properties on the exam. <b>Cohesion</b> (water sticks to water) gives surface tension and lets water be pulled up plant xylem; <b>adhesion</b> (water sticks to other polar surfaces) helps it climb narrow tubes. A <b>high specific heat</b> means water absorbs lots of energy for a small temperature change, so it buffers temperature. Ice is <b>less dense</b> than liquid water because bonds lock molecules into an open lattice, so ice floats. Polar and charged substances dissolve in water; nonpolar ones do not.'],
 ('molecules were attracted only if they were bonded in the ways we used in chemistry','that water has weak but numerous attractions between molecules, not just covalent bonds','why water is liquid at body temperature, floats as ice, and is a solvent','polarity, hydrogen bonding, cohesion, adhesion, specific heat'),
 [('q = m · c · ΔT','heat energy absorbed'),('c(water) = 4.18 J/(g·°C)','high specific heat'),('hydrogen bond: H(δ+) ··· O(δ−)','between molecules'),('polar dissolves polar','"like dissolves like"')],
 [lab('wa-lab','Water molecules at different temperatures',canvas='wa-canvas',aria='Water molecules drawn as particles. Dashed lines show hydrogen bonds between neighbours. Temperature changes how many bonds hold.',
   ctl=[('wa-t','Temperature',-10,110,1,20)],pre='<p class="small" style="margin:0">Dashed lines are hydrogen bonds between neighbouring molecules. Each molecule can hold up to four.</p>',
   out=[('wa-ph','state','x'),('wa-b','hydrogen bonds per molecule (model)','a'),('wa-d','relative density','v')],own=True),
  lab('wa-lab2','Why water resists changes in temperature',canvas='wa-canvas2',aria='Bars comparing the temperature rise of water and cooking oil given the same heat.',
   ctl=[('wa-q','Heat added',100,2000,50,1000),('wa-m','Mass of liquid',20,200,10,100)],
   out=[('wa-dw','temperature rise of water','x'),('wa-do','temperature rise of oil','a')],own=True)],
 [('Slide the temperature from 20 °C down to −5 °C. What happens to the arrangement? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#wa-t",-5]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Molecules lock into a fixed, open pattern with nearly four bonds each. The open lattice holds them farther apart on average than in liquid, so ice is less dense.'),
  ('Raise the temperature to 105 °C. What happens to the bonds? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#wa-t",105]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Molecules move so fast that the attractions cannot hold them: bonds are rarely present and the water is a gas.'),
  ('Add 1000 J to 100 g of each liquid. Which warms more and why? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#wa-q",1000],["#wa-m",100]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Oil warms about twice as much (≈5 °C vs ≈2.4 °C). Water\'s hydrogen bonds absorb some of the energy by being stretched or broken instead of speeding up molecules.')],
 ('How much heat raises 250 g of water from 20 °C to 37 °C? (c = 4.18 J/g°C)',[
  '<b>Formula.</b> q = m · c · ΔT.',
  '<b>ΔT.</b> 37 − 20 = 17 °C.',
  '<b>Substitute.</b> q = 250 × 4.18 × 17.',
  '<b>Answer.</b> ≈ 17,765 J, or about 17.8 kJ. A body that is mostly water therefore warms slowly even when it absorbs lots of heat.']),
 ['Saying a hydrogen bond is a bond inside the water molecule. It is between molecules.','Mixing up cohesion (water to water) and adhesion (water to another surface).','Thinking ice floats because it is "lighter" in some other way. It is less dense because the lattice is more open.','Saying nonpolar molecules dissolve in water.','Treating hydrogen bonds as strong. Each is weak; the effect comes from the number.'],
 ('The links to remember',['Plant xylem transport uses <b>cohesion</b> (a chain of water molecules pulled up) plus <b>adhesion</b> to the wall. Lakes freeze from the top, insulating life below, because ice is less dense. Evaporation of sweat cools you because breaking hydrogen bonds takes energy (<b>high heat of vaporization</b>).']),
 [('Water droplets form beads on a leaf mainly because of',['cohesion between water molecules','covalent bonds inside water','low specific heat','ionic bonds between H and O'],0,'Hydrogen bonds hold the molecules to each other (cohesion), producing surface tension and beads.')])

mod('macromol','Macromolecules & monomers',
 'Cells build enormous molecules out of a few small repeating units, and they take them apart with the same chemistry in reverse.',
 ['There are four classes: <b>carbohydrates</b> (monosaccharides join into polysaccharides: starch, glycogen, cellulose), <b>proteins</b> (amino acids into polypeptides), <b>nucleic acids</b> (nucleotides into DNA and RNA) and <b>lipids</b> (fats are made of glycerol and fatty acids, but lipids are not true polymers). Each has a typical function: energy and structure, catalysis and structure, information, and long-term energy and membranes.',
  'Monomers are joined by <b>dehydration synthesis</b>: each bond forms as a water molecule is removed. Polymers are broken down by <b>hydrolysis</b>, which uses a water molecule to split each bond. The bonds are <b>glycosidic</b> (sugars), <b>peptide</b> (amino acids), <b>phosphodiester</b> (nucleotides) and <b>ester</b> (fats).'],
 ('molecules were either "big" or "small" with no pattern','that big molecules are chains of similar small subunits','how cells build and recycle molecules with one reaction and its reverse','dehydration synthesis, hydrolysis and the four classes'),
 [('n monomers → polymer + (n − 1) H₂O','dehydration synthesis'),('polymer + (n − 1) H₂O → n monomers','hydrolysis'),('carbohydrates: C : H : O ≈ 1 : 2 : 1','empirical ratio'),('proteins contain N; nucleic acids contain N and P','elements')],
 [lab('mm-lab','Build a polymer',canvas='mm-canvas',aria='A chain of monomers with the water molecules released as each bond forms.',
   seg=('mm-kind','Monomer',[('sug','monosaccharide'),('aa','amino acid'),('nuc','nucleotide')]),
   ctl=[('mm-n','Number of monomers',2,12,1,6)],
   out=[('mm-poly','polymer','x'),('mm-bond','bond formed','a'),('mm-h2o','water molecules released','v'),('mm-els','elements','')],own=True)],
 [('Choose amino acids and 10 monomers. How many peptide bonds and waters? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#mm-kind [data-k=aa]"],["#mm-n",10]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','9 peptide bonds and 9 water molecules released: n − 1 of each.'),
  ('Choose nucleotides. Which element appears that carbohydrates lack? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#mm-kind [data-k=nuc]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Phosphorus (in the phosphate of each nucleotide), plus nitrogen from the bases.')],
 ('A polypeptide is built from 150 amino acids. How many water molecules are released during synthesis, and how many are used to break it back into amino acids?',[
  '<b>Bonds.</b> A chain of 150 monomers has 150 − 1 = 149 peptide bonds.',
  '<b>Synthesis.</b> Each bond forms with the removal of one H₂O: 149 waters released.',
  '<b>Hydrolysis.</b> Breaking 149 bonds uses 149 H₂O, the same number returned.']),
 ['Saying hydrolysis removes water. It adds water.','Using n instead of n − 1 for the number of bonds in a linear chain.','Calling lipids polymers. They are not made of repeating identical monomers.','Mixing the names of the bonds.','Forgetting that cellulose and starch are both glucose polymers but differ in how the glucose units are linked, so they behave differently.'],
 ('Structure fits function',['Starch (plants) and glycogen (animals) store glucose; cellulose gives plant cell walls strength. Saturated fats have no double bonds and are solid at room temperature; unsaturated fats have double bonds that kink the chain and are liquid. Phospholipids have a hydrophilic head and hydrophobic tails, which is why they form bilayers.']),
 [('Joining two amino acids by a peptide bond',['releases one water molecule','uses one water molecule','releases carbon dioxide','needs no change in atoms'],0,'Dehydration synthesis removes H₂O as the bond forms.')])

mod('protein','Protein structure',
 'A protein is a chain of amino acids that folds into one particular shape, and the shape is what lets it work.',
 ['Twenty amino acids share the same backbone but differ in their <b>R group</b>: nonpolar (hydrophobic), polar, or charged. The order of amino acids is the <b>primary</b> structure. Hydrogen bonds along the backbone create <b>secondary</b> structure (α-helices and β-pleated sheets). Interactions among R groups (hydrophobic clustering, hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges) fold the chain into a <b>tertiary</b> shape. Some proteins are made of several polypeptides: <b>quaternary</b> structure.',
  'Because folding depends on the R groups, changing even one amino acid can change the shape and function (sickle cell hemoglobin has one substitution). Heat, pH and salt can break the weak interactions that hold the shape: that is <b>denaturation</b>, and it usually destroys function.'],
 ('proteins were just long chains','that the chain folds into a precise 3-D shape that depends on its sequence','why sequence changes and denaturation affect function','four levels of structure and what holds each one'),
 [('primary: amino acid sequence','peptide bonds'),('secondary: α-helix, β-sheet','hydrogen bonds in the backbone'),('tertiary: overall fold','R-group interactions'),('quaternary: several chains','same kinds of interactions')],
 [stagelab('pr-lab','Four levels of protein structure',canvas='pr-canvas',aria='A diagram that changes with each level: a chain of beads, a helix, a folded blob and a complex of subunits.',p='pr',n=4,out_extra=[('pr-bond','held together by','v')])],
 [('Go to the tertiary stage. What are the main forces? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#pr-st",2]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Interactions between R groups: hydrophobic clustering inside, hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and covalent disulfide bridges between cysteines.'),
  ('At the quaternary stage, what is new? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#pr-st",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','More than one polypeptide chain assembles into one functional protein, as in hemoglobin (four chains).')],
 ('Sickle cell hemoglobin has valine instead of glutamic acid at position 6 of the β chain. Explain how one change can change the protein.',[
  '<b>Primary change.</b> Glutamic acid has a charged R group; valine is nonpolar.',
  '<b>Effect on interactions.</b> The new nonpolar R group sticks to hydrophobic spots on other hemoglobin molecules.',
  '<b>Effect on structure.</b> Molecules clump into long fibers at low oxygen, changing the red blood cell shape.',
  '<b>Conclusion.</b> A single change in primary structure altered interactions, shape and function.']),
 ['Saying peptide bonds hold the tertiary structure. They hold the primary structure; weak interactions and disulfides fold the chain.','Thinking denaturation breaks peptide bonds. It disrupts folding but usually leaves the sequence intact.','Forgetting that the R group determines where an amino acid sits in the folded protein.','Mixing secondary (backbone H-bonds) with tertiary (R-group interactions).','Assuming every protein has quaternary structure.'],
 ('Reading a data question',['If a mutation swaps a polar amino acid for a nonpolar one, predict where it will be (more likely inside, or on a surface where it disrupts solubility) and whether folding or binding is affected. Use "R group", "interaction", "shape" and "function" in your explanation.']),
 [('Which level of protein structure is the linear sequence of amino acids?',['primary','secondary','tertiary','quaternary'],0,'Primary is the order of amino acids joined by peptide bonds.')])

mod('dnastruct','DNA & RNA structure',
 'DNA stores biological information in a simple pattern: two strands, four bases, and fixed pairing rules.',
 ['A <b>nucleotide</b> has a sugar (deoxyribose in DNA, ribose in RNA), a phosphate and a nitrogenous base. In DNA the bases are adenine (A), thymine (T), guanine (G) and cytosine (C). Nucleotides join by <b>phosphodiester bonds</b> between the sugar of one and the phosphate of the next, forming a sugar-phosphate backbone with a direction: one end is 5′ and the other is 3′.',
  'Two strands run <b>antiparallel</b> and are held by hydrogen bonds between bases: A pairs with T (two bonds) and G pairs with C (three bonds). So in double-stranded DNA, %A = %T and %G = %C (Chargaff\'s rules). RNA is usually single-stranded, uses ribose, and has uracil (U) in place of thymine.'],
 ('DNA was the molecule of heredity, little more','that its structure explains how information is stored and copied','why the amount of A equals T and why G–C rich DNA is harder to melt','nucleotides, base pairing, antiparallel strands'),
 [('A = T and G = C in double-stranded DNA','Chargaff'),('A–T: 2 H-bonds; G–C: 3 H-bonds','pairing'),('strands run 5′ → 3′ and 3′ → 5′','antiparallel'),('RNA: ribose, U instead of T','single-stranded')],
 [lab('dn-lab','Base composition of double-stranded DNA',canvas='dn-canvas',aria='A double helix drawn with the proportion of GC and AT pairs set by a slider. Counts of each base are shown.',
   ctl=[('dn-n','Length',20,200,10,100),('dn-gc','GC content',20,80,1,40)],
   out=[('dn-a','A','x'),('dn-t','T','x'),('dn-g','G','a'),('dn-c','C','a'),('dn-hb','hydrogen bonds in total','v'),('dn-tm','how hard to melt','')],own=True)],
 [('Set GC content to 70%. Which is harder to separate: this or 30% GC? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#dn-gc",70]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','70% GC. G–C pairs have three hydrogen bonds versus two for A–T, so more energy (a higher temperature) is needed to separate the strands.'),
  ('If the GC content is 40%, what are the percentages of A and T? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#dn-gc",40]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','30% each: A = T and the rest (100 − 40 = 60%) splits evenly between A and T.')],
 ('A double-stranded DNA sample is 22% cytosine. What percentage is adenine? How many hydrogen bonds hold a 100 bp segment of it together?',[
  '<b>Chargaff.</b> C = G = 22%, so G + C = 44%.',
  '<b>A and T.</b> The remaining 56% is split equally: A = T = 28%.',
  '<b>Hydrogen bonds in 100 bp.</b> 44 G–C pairs × 3 = 132 and 56 A–T pairs × 2 = 112, total 244.']),
 ['Writing that A pairs with U in DNA.','Believing the strands run in the same direction.','Forgetting G–C has three bonds and A–T two.','Saying %A + %G = 50% is the same as %A = %G.','Mixing up deoxyribose and ribose.'],
 ('Applying Chargaff',['If you are given the percent of one base in double-stranded DNA you can find all four. In single-stranded DNA or RNA the rules do not apply. Compare melting temperatures by comparing G + C percentage: higher GC means more bonds and a higher melting temperature.']),
 [('In double-stranded DNA, if 30% of the bases are thymine, what percent are guanine?',['20%','30%','40%','70%'],0,'T = A = 30%, so G + C = 40% and G = 20%.')])
